{"body": "Memo of 11 Jan 2021, from months of debate with his son Andrew (a growth/tech investor) during the pandemic. Context: growth had beaten value for ~13 years. Conclusions: 'carrying low valuation parameters' is 'far from synonymous with \"underpriced\"'; 'a stock with a low p/e ratio, for example, is likely to be a bargain only if its current earnings and recent earnings growth are indicative of the future.' Pure low-multiple pursuit leads to 'value traps': things that look cheap 'because they have operating weaknesses or because the sales and earnings creating those valuations can't be replicated.' On edge: 'Since quantitative information regarding the present is so readily available, success in the highly competitive field of investing is more likely to be the result of superior judgments about qualitative factors and future events'; 'successful investing has to be more about superior judgments concerning (a) qualitative, non-computable factors and (b) how things are likely to unfold in the future.' Marks admits Oaktree's Graham-style caution may have cost returns in winners-take-most industries. Actionable: public screens (low P/E, high ROE on screener.in) carry no edge by themselves; edge comes from scuttlebutt, industry structure and variant perception about the future.", "confidence": "verified", "created": "2026-11-20T03:41:28Z", "data": {"memo_date": "2021-01-11"}, "date": "2021-01-11", "id": "insight:marks-something-of-value-2021", "links": [{"rel": "about", "to": "person:howard-marks"}, {"rel": "part_of", "to": "strategy:marks-cycle-temperature"}, {"rel": "about", "to": "strategy:variant-perception"}, {"rel": "about", "to": "strategy:fisher-scuttlebutt"}], "sources": ["https://www.oaktreecapital.com/insights/memo/something-of-value"], "tags": ["marks", "valuation", "value-trap", "edge", "growth", "process", "global"], "title": "Marks Jan-2021 'Something of Value': low multiples are not value; edge lives in qualitative, forward judgement", "type": "insight", "updated": "2026-11-20T03:41:28Z", "version": 1}
{"body": "Same-method promise scores for five Indian auto-component makers (last 6 concalls each, Aug-2025 to Nov-2026; UNIPARTS from its 10-call pilot). Weighted delivery (credit, specificity-weighted): SONACOMS 83.3% (n=6), SANSERA 60.0% (n=8), HAPPYFORGE 50.0% (n=7), ENDURANCE 26.1% (n=9), UNIPARTS 64.8% (n=47). Sector pattern: group revenue and margin guidance is usually met (pooled revenue 68.8%, margin 80.6%); capacity/SOP dates are the weak category (pooled 43.3%), with 1-5 quarter slips at Endurance, Happy Forgings and Sansera - the same date-slip pattern seen in T&D (L18). Two of five (Sona, Endurance) refuse revenue guidance, so their scored records are mostly mix statements or SOP dates; categories rarely reach n>=5, so SAC is computed with small categories pooled. SAC (pts vs leave-one-out peer medians): SONACOMS +50.0, SANSERA -4.8, HAPPYFORGE +3.6, ENDURANCE -43.0, UNIPARTS -8.1. Use: base rates by category for credibility-generated paper calls (thesis tag credibility-generated). n is small (6-9 scored per new company): provisional. Research, not advice.", "confidence": "single-source", "created": "2026-11-22T12:02:33Z", "data": {"adjustment_rule": "p0 = leave-one-out pooled STRICT delivery rate (specificity-weighted share 'delivered'; partly=0) of the other 4 peers in the promise's category (all-category peer rate if <8 peer promises in that category). p = clip(p0 + N/(N+20) x SAC/100, 0.05, 0.95), N = company's scored promises, SAC in points (credit basis: delivered 1, partly 0.5; categories with n<5, or with <2 peers having data, pooled). P(base) = p0.", "as_of": "2026-11-22", "base_rates_leave_one_out": {"ENDURANCE": {"_all": {"n": 68, "p0_pct": 56.7}, "capacity": {"fallback_all": false, "n": 13, "p0_pct": 46.7}, "margin": {"fallback_all": false, "n": 9, "p0_pct": 76.5}, "other": {"fallback_all": false, "n": 18, "p0_pct": 44.7}, "revenue_growth": {"fallback_all": false, "n": 28, "p0_pct": 64.3}}, "HAPPYFORGE": {"_all": {"n": 70, "p0_pct": 53.8}, "c